<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Occupied Japan</h1><p class="st-meta">1945 CE – 1952 CE · capital Tokyo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Allied occupation of Japan after its defeat in 1945, led by General Douglas MacArthur, which disarmed the country and rebuilt it as a democracy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration">Potsdam Declaration</a> on 15 August 1945 and signed the instrument of surrender on 2 September aboard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uss-missouri">USS Missouri</a>. Allied, overwhelmingly American, forces occupied the home islands under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</a>, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The occupation ruled indirectly through the Japanese government and the emperor, who renounced his divinity. It disbanded the armed forces, tried war leaders at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Tokyo</a> tribunal, purged officials, broke up the zaibatsu, carried out a sweeping land reform, gave women the vote and in 1947 introduced a new constitution renouncing war. From 1947 a reverse course favoured economic recovery and anti-communism, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> of 1950 turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> into a supply base.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco">San Francisco Peace Treaty</a>, signed in 1951, took effect on 28 April 1952, restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> alongside a security treaty with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa</a> remained under American control.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/occupied-japan.jpg" alt="Occupied Japan" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Scott Alter (Scottalter) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Allied_Occupied_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1945 CE – 1952 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tokyo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Douglas MacArthur, Matthew Ridgway, Yoshida Shigeru</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shinto, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Occupation of Japan, Allied occupation of Japan, SCAP Japan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1948&amp;focus=occupied-japan" aria-label="Occupied Japan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8207 888 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Occupied Japan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8527 1144L8529 1145M8527 1144L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8529 1145h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8527 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8527 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tokyo. <a href="/atlas?year=1948&amp;focus=occupied-japan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
